# A task spawned as an immediately called lambda - "spawn fn() { ... }()" inside a loop - that captures two references
# (here two arrays given to the function) and the loop's variable sees wrong captures: some tasks write another
# iteration's part, some parts are never written. The same lambda made once outside the join and spawned as
# "spawn body(t)" is right every time, and "spawn fn() { ... }" (without the call) is refused for capturing the loop's
# variable (P2, D16e) - so the called form seems to slip past that check, its closure living where a join-local
# variable does. With one captured reference it came out right in every run tried. Found moving attention onto a Gemm
# per head (ops.olang), where it gave wrong products for a quarter to a half of the elements.
# Expected: "wrong elements: 0 of 25600" (or a compile-time error, as for the uncalled form). Actual (build with -b
# and run, a few times): hundreds to thousands wrong. Workaround: ops.parallel's form - the lambda made outside the
# join, the loop spawning a call of it.
# Status: reproduces on olang 472373d (6912, 2816 and 10752 wrong in three runs); being changed in olang (wt-cgfix3).
fn slow(n I64) I64 {
    s I64 = 0
    for i in range n { s = s + i % 7 }
    return s
}

fn run(a Array<I64>&, c mut Array<I64>&, n I64) {
    join {
        for t in range n {
            spawn fn() {
                w := slow(200000)
                for i in range 64 { c[t * 64 + i] = a[t] + w * 0 }
            }()
        }
    }
}

fn main() ? {
    n I64 = 4
    bad I64 = 0
    for trial in range 100 {
        a := I64[0, 1, 2, 3]
        c := Array<I64>(64 * n)
        run(a, c, n)
        for i in range c.Len() {
            if c[i] != i / 64 { bad++ }
        }
    }
    println("wrong elements: " $bad " of " $(100 * 64 * n))
}
